            "content": [
              {
                "annotations": [],
                "text": "An analysis of the examples reveals the following transformation rule:\n\n1. **Grid Dimensions:** The output grid has the exact same dimensions (height $H$, width $W$) as the input grid.\n2. **Background:** Every cell that is not colored `1` becomes `8` (teal).\n3. **Movement of the `1`:** \n   - Looking from the bottom row ($r = H - 1$) up to the top row ($r = 0$), the `1` starts at column 0 in the bottom row (as indicated by the input grid where `1` is at `(H-1, 0)`).\n   - Moving upwards row-by-row (from $r = H - 1$ to $0$), the position of `1` bounces back and forth between column 0 and column $W - 1$.\n   - Specifically, starting at the bottom row $r = H - 1$, the column index is $c = 0$.\n   - Moving up to the previous row, the column index bounces: $0 \\to 1 \\to 2 \\dots \\to W-1 \\to W-2 \\dots \\to 0 \\to \\dots$\n   - Formally, let $d$ be the distance from the bottom row, $d = (H - 1) - r$. The cycle period is $2(W - 1)$ (or 2 if $W=2$).\n   - For a given row $r$, let $d = H - 1 - r$.\n   - Let $m = d \\pmod{2W - 2}$ (if $W > 1$).\n   - Then the column position of `1` is:\n     $$c(r) = \\begin{cases} m & \\text{if } m \\le W - 1 \\\\ 2(W - 1) - m & \\text{if } m > W - 1 \\end{cases}$$\n   - In row $r$, cell $(r, c(r))$ is `1`, and all other cells in row $r$ are `8`.\n\nI am ready for the test input.",
